{"id": "03e154b6-f88b-49bd-9ea8-fb6fd72d62dc", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:33:57.998450", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "25861ee6-1d1e-4d14-bbb2-159ecaa658f3", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "No WorkArena++ code, no configs, no LLM client, no task taxonomy. Only task.json. Network disabled, no API keys, no GPU (task says gpu_enabled: false, though CUDA dir exists — it's a base image artifact; no torch installed).\n\nThe researcher references researcher_topics: \"neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1\". That's a paper reference — NeurIPS 2024 paper #97713. I don't have the paper text in the repo. The topic id suggests a \"setup\" section chunk. I should note that I don't have access to that source document in /workspace; my knowledge of WorkArena++ comes from prior knowledge (assumption/assumption-class), not from repo-verified source.\n\nWhat do I know about WorkArena++? WorkArena (Drouin et al., NeurIPS 2024) is a benchmark of interactive browser-based enterprise tasks built on ServiceNow, with the AgentAscent API / AgentBench? Actually WorkArena is built on the ServiceNow platform using the AgentAscent API; it includes ~29 tasks (WorkArena) plus WebArena and VisualWebArena integration in WorkArena++. WorkArena++ = \"augmented multi-protocol, multi-application, and long-distance real-world workflow benchmark\" — it extends WorkArena with multi-tab/multi-application workflows, includes tasks across single-tab and multi-tab setups, with a taxonomy of task difficulty levels (it, mt, wt?). Actually I recall the WorkArena++ paper (\"WorkArena: Moving Towards AGI for Automation of Knowledge Work Workflows\", Drouin et al., NeurIPS 2024, arXiv:2407.12971) which introduced WorkArena++ with multi-tab tasks, and the Coxbase/AssistantWave agents. The task taxonomy in WorkArena includes categories like \"it\", \"sys_db\", \"cmdb\", \"knowledge\" etc. There's an \"oracle\" success metric.\n\nThe researcher's mention of \"work_area/symlink टैक्सनम\" — hmm, \"work_area/symlink\" — maybe that's a hint about the actual taxonomy in a masked repo? Actually \"work_area\" and \"symlink\" seem like task taxonomy categories in some harness. Hmm, this may be a generic hallucination-trap: the researcher suggests \"e.g. success rate on work_area/symlink taxonomy\". WorkArena does have task types relating to \"symlink\"? Unclear. Actually, in WorkArena, tasks are generated with config names like \"browserui_workarena_...\" Hmm.\n\nWait — there might be a real connection: WorkArena's task suite has \"symlink\" tasks? I'm not sure. I recall the WorkArena config includes tasks like \"sm\" (service management), \"it\", \"pa\" (problem), \"chn\", \"ctask\"... and \"symlink\" is not standard ServiceNow jargon. Hmm, actually there IS something: in AgentAscent/WorkArena, tasks are organized under directories like \"config/work_area/symlink/...\"? Let me be careful: I should treat the taxonomy as unknown/needs-verification rather than asserting.\n\nI should be honest: I cannot verify the taxonomy from any source in /workspace. I'll flag the \"work_area/symlink\" taxonomy as a claim from the researcher's prompt, not repo-verified, and mark it as needing researcher/source confirmation (topic neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1 presumably contains it).\n\nAlso note \"C\" — WorkArena++ has \"WorkArena\" and \"WorkArena++\" with the latter's harder, longer-horizon, multi-application tasks; agents: Coxbase, and models evaluated: GPT-4o, GPT-4-Turbo, Claude-3-Opus, Mixtral, CodeS? The paper reports success rates quite low (~5% for best agent?). But all this is prior knowledge, not repo source.\n\nNow, the deliverable for this turn: stage=plan with (1) environment inspection findings, (2) proposed approach outline (not full design), (3) explicit fact vs. assumption separation, plus how the design addresses disentangling CoT benefit from task difficulty and prompt-length/verbosity confounds.\n\nKey design ideas for disentangling:\n- Conditions: (A) no-CoT (direct action output / structured action only), (B) CoT (free-form reasoning before action, same action schema), (C) length/verbosity-controlled active control: e.g., \"concise CoT\" with fixed token budget (reasoning limited to N tokens), or a \"pseudoreasoning\" control where the model emits a fixed-length filler/non-informative scratchpad (e.g., restate the observation / write a generic template of the same token length), or \"post-hoc rationale\" (rationale generated after the action, not fed back into the policy). Post-hoc rationale control is the classic way to separate \"extra tokens/compute before action\" from \"recurrent reasoning that conditions the action\".\n- Actually a cleaner decomposition: (1) no-CoT, (2) CoT with reasoning before action, (3) same total token budget with non-causal content (scrambled/shuffled rationale, or rationale about an unrelated topic, or rationale placed after action), (4) longer non-reasoning prefix (padding with repeated task description) — controls for prompt-length effects on attention/position.\n- Compute-matched control: compare no-CoT with best-of-N sampling without rationale vs CoT greedy — separates \"more inference compute\" from \"verbalized reasoning\".\n- Also: within-condition, regress/stratify success delta on prompt token count and rationale length (matched-pairs on trajectory token count); report the effect at matched total tokens via token-matched subset or a mixed model with log-token covariate.\n- Task difficulty stratification: use WorkArena++ native strata (single-tab vs multi-tab / multi-app, oracle-horizon length i.e. minimum number of steps, number of applications, taxonomy category). Pre-register difficulty proxies: ground-truth minimal action sequence length, number of distinct apps/tabs, taxonomy family. Then test interaction CoT × difficulty — expecting CoT helps (or hurts) more on long-horizon tasks; report stratum-specific effects rather than a single pooled average. Use difficulty as a blocking factor (stratified randomization / crossover within task-family).\n- Crossover / paired design: the same task instance (seeded config) run in both conditions → paired binary outcomes → McNemar's exact test + stratified (Cochran–Mantel–Haenszel) across difficulty strata; this controls task-instance difficulty perfectly. Task-level random effects via GLMM (logit success ~ condition + difficulty + condition:difficulty + (1|task)).\n- Determinism/decoding: temperature 0 (greedy) for the primary comparison to reduce variance; plus temperature >0 replicates (n seeds) for secondary robustness; identical model snapshot/version pinned, identical system prompt/action space/observation format, identical max steps, identical tool/action API, identical observation truncation, identical time budget.\n- Agent scaffolding fixed (e.g., the benchmark's default ReAct-style agent, CoT condition differs ONLY by whether a reasoning field precedes the action field in the output schema). Emphasize \"minimal-pair prompting\": the only difference is presence/absence of reasoning tokens; keep instruction text identical to avoid prompt-edit confounds.\n- Order/counterbalancing: because each task runs once per condition, counterbalance condition order across tasks (ABBA) to protect against drift in external API or environment state; randomize task execution order; ensure environment reset/isolation per episode (fresh browser profile, seeded data).\n- Metrics: primary = task success rate per benchmark oracle (binary), computed as mean over tasks, and paired difference; secondary = partial progress (oracle sub-goal / checkpoint), steps-to-success, invalid/malformed action rate, timeout/step-limit exhaustion rate, trajectory token cost, latency, and cost-normalized success (success per 1K tokens). Also report \"regression\" risk: tasks solved by no-CoT but failed by CoT.\n- Budget: fixed max agent steps per task from the benchmark default; total step/token budget cap per condition; episode timeout.\n- Analysis: pre-registered primary endpoint; equivalence/margins; multiple-comparison correction across strata; report CIs; power: N per condition determined by expected McNemar discordance rate — but I cannot fix N without knowing expected effect (list as open parameter, provide a sensitivity/power formula).\n- Feasibility in this environment: no network, no deps → the design is a paper design; can pre-build a small offline analysis harness skeleton? Not required. Note that the design must state which external resources are needed: WorkArena++ task suite + served ServiceNow-like environment, model endpoint.\n\nAlso honest: \"improve\" needs operationalization; and a null result may be environment/version-specific.\n\nI should also note the \"no-CoT\" in agentic settings is ambiguous: even without an explicit reasoning field, the model's single-token action selection embeds implicit reasoning; and CoT in an agentic loop has a second-order effect: rationales enter the conversation history and affect future steps. Need to control history-inclusion: variant that includes rationale in history vs. strips it (keeps per-step independence). That's an important clean design point.\n\nFact vs assumption:\nFacts (verified in this environment): /workspace contains only task.json (415 bytes) with question/context/documents; documents track.md says design-only, repos and compute not required. Python 3.14.4 at /usr/bin/python3, 105 pip packages, none related to WorkArena/agents/LLM clients (only `requests`/`requests_ntlm` as HTTP); no API keys in env; DNS resolution fails → network disabled; no GPU enabled per task spec; no datasets listed; no WorkArena++ code/config/taxonomy/logging scaffolding anywhere on the searched paths (/opt, /usr/local, /srv, /home, /root, /tmp).\nAssumptions (not verified): WorkArena++ specifics — that it is a ServiceNow-based browser benchmark with multi-app/multi-tab long-horizon tasks and programmatic oracle success; that a \"work_area/symlink\" taxonomy exists (came from