hed: restrict to task×seed pairs where the CoT arm's completion tokens are within ±X% of control arm's... that's a post-treatment selection issue — must be honest: matching on a post-treatment variable (per-step token count) can induce collider bias. Better: pre-register per-step token cap arms (length-capped CoT) as the causal approach, and use the filler arm as the main defensor; regression adjustment on cumulative length labeled as mediator-adjustment / descriptive, not causal (do not over-claim). Good honesty point.\n- A3 dose-response: trend test across length-capped arms (≤50 / ≤150 words) + no-CoT + filler — test whether success increases with rationale budget at fixed prompt; slope of success vs rationale-token-budget; if the effect scales with rationale budget beyond the filler's matched length, that's evidence of reasoning-specific effect.\n- A4: history-ablation variant: strip rationale from subsequent-step context (rationale generated but not fed back) → separates \"extra computation at this step\" from \"rationale-as-memory\". That's a nice arm — actually it's the mechanism decomposition: within-step compute vs cross-step memory. Put this as optional/stretch arm (post-hoc rationale partially covers it; the \"strip-rationale-from-history\" arm is different and cheap). I'll list post-hoc rationale as required-ish optional tier 2 and history-stripping as tier 2 too. The researcher said post-hoc rationale, length-capped, best-of-n = optional/stretch. So I'll keep tiering exactly as the researcher said: tier 1 = no-CoT format-matched, CoT, verbosity-control; tier 2 optional = post-hoc rationale, length-capped dose, best-of-n; and I can add history-stripping as a noted tier-2 addition (labeled as my addition, optional).\n\nEnvironment reset caveat: pairing depends on identical state reset. If identical reset not verifiable, fallback design: block-matched randomization — pre-test difficulty via pilot on a calibration subset, stratify by measured baseline; pairing downgrades to matched-pairs with task random intercept; McNemar invalid → use cluster-robust/CMH within blocks. Must state \"design fails in this way\".\n\nAlso add: deterministic environment fingerprint check — hash environment state at episode start (URL/DOM snapshot hash of initial page + seeded data version) as a check; if hashes differ, flag pair as broken and pre-register sensitivity excluding broken pairs.\n\nArms per-step protocol details: same system prompt, same action schema (JSON), same observation rendering (accessibility tree / text), same truncation policy, same max steps, same tool set, model pinned, temperature 0 primary, prompt caching off, log full transcripts.\n\nRandomization/counterbalancing: within task, condition order ABBA across the condition set; task order randomized by seeded permutation; arms assigned by a seeded random schedule stored in manifest.\n\nMetrics: primary = binary success (validator) ITT; secondary = partial credit, steps to success, invalid-action rate, timeout rate, cumulative prompt+completion tokens, cost per success, latency, trajectory divergence.\n\nPlaceholders table with UNSPECIFIED tag and feasibility impact: model family/version, N tasks, N seeds, step cap, per-step token cap, timeout, budget/GPU, scaffold baseline, published results reuse, difficulty metadata availability, reset-identity.\n\nAnalysis: mixed logistic: success ~ condition + stratum + condition×stratum + log(cumulative tokens) + (1|task) + (1|task:condition?) with seeds as replicate; report OR and risk difference; CMH stratified; power/simulation: N needed depends on discordance rate; provide formula for McNemar: n_discordant needed = ((z_{1-α}+z_{1-β})^2)/(p10-p01)^2... provide as an equation, with p10/p01 UNSPECIFIED pilot quantities.\n\nAlso note: with temp=0 and identical scaffold, the no-CoT and CoT arms differ; determinism check needed: model endpoint determinism UNSPECIFIED — run 2 replicate identical-condition episodes to measure flip rate; if flip rate > 0, treat as seed-level replicate.\n\nLet me now run a few quick checks (pip cache, disk, venv) and then produce the final result handoff.\n\nAlso note: the answer should be in the researcher's language register (Hinglish/Bengali-mixed Hindi). I'll write mostly in the same Hinglish style with English technical terms, matching their script style. I should keep it readable. The researcher writes in Devanagari with Latin technical terms. I'll do the same.\n\nLength: this is a full design document — should be thorough but organized. Let me write it.\n\nLet me do the final checks.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "echo \"--- disk ---\"; df -h /workspace /tmp / 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"--- pip cache ---\"; python3 -m pip cache dir 2>/dev/null; python3 -m pip cache info 2>/dev/null | head -5; echo \"--- venv test ---\"; python3 -m venv /tmp/venvtest 2>&1 | tail -2; ls /tmp/venvtest/bin 2>/dev/null | head -5", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b0bbb02589c917dc", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b0bbb02589c917dc", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"echo \\\"--- disk ---\\\"; df -h /workspace /tmp / 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"--- pip cache ---\\\"; python3 -m pip cache dir 2>/dev/null; python3 -m pip cache info 2>/dev/null | head -5; echo \\\"--- venv test ---\\\"; python3 -m venv /tmp/venvtest 2>&1 | tail -2; ls /tmp/venvtest/bin 2>/dev/null | head -5\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1604d4ed-1678-47d5-9620-3cdfad014855", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "execute_check: {\"command\": \"echo \\\"--- disk ---\\\"; df -h /workspace /tmp / 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"--- pip cache ---\\\"; python3 -m pip cache dir 2>/dev/null; python3 -m pip cache info 2>/dev/null | head -5; echo \\\"--- venv test ---\\\"; python3 -m venv /t